item.content:encoded:
I build navigation software for drones that can't rely on GPS. Whenever I explain that work, I end up explaining the same thing first: where path planning sits in an autonomous system, and why it's harder than "find the shortest route." This is that explanation, written once, properly.The examples lean on self-driving cars because that's where the literature is deepest. Everything here carries over to drones, ground robots, and boats, with a few differences I'll cover near the end.Why autonomy, and why nowAutonomous driving is an old idea. Experiments with radio-controlled "driverless" cars date back to the 1920s. What changed in the last decade is that sensors became cheap, compute moved to the edge, and deep learning made perception actually work.The safety argument is the one you hear most. You'll often see the claim that around 94% of crashes are caused by human error. That number comes from NHTSA, and NHTSA itself warns that it describes the last failure in the chain before a crash, not blame. The direction is still right: most crashes involve a human misjudgement somewhere, and a machine that never gets tired or distracted has room to do better. "Room to do better" is the honest version.The stack in sixty secondsMost autonomous driving systems are described as six layers:: cameras, LiDAR, radar, IMU, GNSS collect raw data.: turns raw data into objects, lanes, and free space.: works out where the vehicle is in the world.: estimates danger and predicts what other road users will do.Path planning & decision-making: chooses where to go and how.: turns the plan into steering, throttle, and braking.The planner sits at layer five, which means it inherits every mistake made above it. A good planner is therefore as much about handling uncertain inputs as it is about clever search. So before getting to planning, here's what it's being fed.What the planner sees: sensorsColour, texture, semantics; cheapNo direct depth; struggles at night and in harsh shadowsDepth from two cameras a known baseline apartFails on low-texture surfaces; accuracy drops with distanceStructured light / ToF cameraDepth via projected IR pattern or light round-trip timeShort range; sunlight interference outdoorsPer-pixel brightness changes, asynchronously; microsecond latency, ~120 dB dynamic rangeUnusual data format; tooling still maturing360° view from fisheye arraysNeeds careful multi-camera calibrationRange and relative velocity, robust in rain and fogDense 3D point clouds from laser pulsesCost; degraded by heavy rain, fog, dustRadar and LiDAR share a basic idea: emit a signal and measure what comes back. FMCW radar sweeps the frequency of radio waves and computes range from the frequency difference between the transmitted and returned signal. LiDAR uses infrared laser light, either scanned mechanically or, increasingly, with solid-state and flash designs that have no spinning parts.No sensor is good at everything, which is why production vehicles fuse several. That fusion is also the first place uncertainty enters the planner's world.What the planner sees: perceptionPerception converts sensor data into things a planner can reason about: finds cars, pedestrians, signs, and lights in images and draws bounding boxes around them, usually with CNN- or transformer-based detectors. comes in two flavours.  segmentation labels every pixel with a class ("road", "car").  segmentation goes further and separates individual objects, giving each car its own pixel mask. adds depth from stereo, LiDAR, or radar. Grouping sparse 3D points into clean objects is still hard. follows moving objects over time to estimate their velocity and heading. This is what lets the system predict where things will be, not just where they are. Fusing multiple sensors here, often into an occupancy grid, is standard. is its own problem because lanes are continuous surfaces, not objects. The usual pipeline is: preprocess the raw data, extract lane features, fit continuous curves (splines and similar models), then smooth the result over time using the vehicle's motion.What the planner sees: localisationThe planner needs to know where the vehicle is to within centimetres. Three approaches dominate: The IMU (gyroscope, accelerometer, magnetometer) tracks motion but drifts over time. GNSS corrects the drift but is only accurate to a few metres without augmentation, and it vanishes in tunnels and urban canyons. Good as an initial estimate, not enough on its own.SLAM (Simultaneous Localisation and Mapping) builds a map and localises within it at the same time.  uses cameras and is cheap and rich in landmarks but sensitive to lighting and texture.  produces accurate point-cloud maps at higher cost. I've written separately about building a VSLAM pipeline matches live sensor data against a pre-built high-definition map, either by point-cloud matching or by recognising known landmarks such as signs and road markings.This layer is personal for me. My company builds a vision-only navigation SDK for drones in GPS-denied environments, and the core lesson from that work is simple: a planner is only as good as the pose estimate it's given. If localisation is off by a metre, a "collision-free" path isn't.What the planner sees: risk and intentThe assessment layer asks two questions: how dangerous is the current scene, and what are the other agents about to do?That covers detecting unsafe lane changes, predicting cut-ins, and classifying driving styles (an aggressive driver behind you behaves differently from a cautious one). Older systems used clustering methods such as PCA and k-means, or SVMs and small neural networks. Modern stacks increasingly use learned trajectory-prediction models that output several possible futures for each agent, with probabilities.Those multiple futures are the planner's most important input, and its biggest headache.Here's the core of the article. "Path planning" is really three nested problems running at different speeds.1. Global (route) planningWhich roads take me from A to B? This runs on a road graph built from an offline map, recalculates rarely, and is basically solved. Dijkstra's algorithm and A* handle it, with tricks such as contraction hierarchies making continent-scale routing fast.One correction to a claim I made in the original version of this article: finding the shortest path on a graph is not NP-hard. Dijkstra solves it in polynomial time. What's genuinely hard is  in continuous space, which has been known since the late 1970s to be PSPACE-hard. That's where the difficulty lives, in the next two layers.Given the route and the scene, what should the vehicle ? Keep the lane, change lanes, yield, overtake, stop, creep forward at an occluded junction. This used to be done with finite state machines and rule trees. It's increasingly learned or handled by search over candidate manoeuvres, because rules explode in number once you hit real traffic.3. Local (motion) planningGiven the chosen behaviour, what exact trajectory should the vehicle follow for the next few seconds? It must be:, against obstacles that move and react;, meaning a car can't turn on the spot, and curvature and acceleration have limits;, with low jerk and no wobbling;, typically replanned every 50–100 ms.This is where most of the engineering effort goes.Graph search on a discretised world. Chop the space into a grid or lattice and search it. A* is the workhorse: it explores nodes in order of cost so far + estimated cost to go, and with an admissible heuristic it's guaranteed to find the optimal path. Here's a minimal version on a 4-connected grid:import heapq

def astar(grid, start, goal):
    """4-connected grid A*. grid[r][c] == 1 means blocked."""
    def h(p):  # Manhattan distance: admissible on a 4-connected grid
        return abs(p[0] - goal[0]) + abs(p[1] - goal[1])

    open_heap = [(h(start), 0, start)]
    came_from = {start: None}
    g = {start: 0}

    while open_heap:
        _, cost, node = heapq.heappop(open_heap)
        if node == goal:
            path = []
            while node is not None:
                path.append(node)
                node = came_from[node]
            return path[::-1]
        if cost > g[node]:
            continue  # stale heap entry
        r, c = node
        for nr, nc in ((r + 1, c), (r - 1, c), (r, c + 1), (r, c - 1)):
            if 0 <= nr < len(grid) and 0 <= nc < len(grid[0]) and grid[nr][nc] == 0:
                new_cost = cost + 1
                if new_cost < g.get((nr, nc), float("inf")):
                    g[(nr, nc)] = new_cost
                    came_from[(nr, nc)] = node
                    heapq.heappush(open_heap, (new_cost + h((nr, nc)), new_cost, (nr, nc)))
    return None
Grid A* ignores vehicle dynamics, so cars use , which searches over continuous position and heading using motion primitives a real vehicle can drive. It was made famous by Stanford's entry in the 2007 DARPA Urban Challenge and is still common in parking and low-speed manoeuvring. Instead of discretising everything, randomly sample the space and connect feasible samples.  grows a tree quickly toward unexplored space;  adds rewiring so the path converges toward optimal as samples increase;  builds a reusable roadmap. These shine in high-dimensional or cluttered spaces, which is why they're popular for drones and robot arms. The trade-off is jittery paths that need smoothing.Lattice and Frenét-frame planners. On structured roads, express motion relative to the lane centreline (along-road distance and lateral offset), generate a fan of candidate trajectories, score each one for safety, comfort, and progress, and pick the best. Simple, fast, interpretable, and widely used in highway driving.Optimisation-based planning. Treat the trajectory as an optimisation problem: minimise a cost (deviation, jerk, time) subject to constraints (dynamics, collision avoidance). Model Predictive Control (MPC) solves this repeatedly over a short receding horizon, so it naturally blurs the line between planning and control. The catch is that collision constraints are non-convex, so solvers need good initial guesses, often supplied by one of the methods above. Train a network to output trajectories directly, usually by imitating expert driving and increasingly refined with reinforcement learning. Since 2024, the most active line of research has been , which generate trajectories through iterative denoising. They're appealing because driving is multimodal: at a junction, "go left" and "go right" are both valid, and regression-based models tend to average the two into "go straight into the wall." DiffusionDrive (CVPR 2025) and Diffusion Planner (ICLR 2025) are good entry points.In practice, production systems mix these: search or sampling for a coarse answer, optimisation to make it smooth and feasible, and learned components to predict others and score options.The 2025–26 shift: end-to-endThe biggest change since I first wrote about this topic in mid-2025 is how far the industry moved toward  systems, where perception, prediction, and planning are trained together, from sensor input to trajectory output.Tesla replaced most of its hand-written planning code with neural networks in FSD v12. Waymo published EMMA in 2024, a research model built on Gemini that maps camera input directly to planner trajectories. Then in late 2025 Waymo described its production driver as a foundation model trained end-to-end. Waymo's framing is the useful one: end-to-end is a training strategy, not an architecture. You can keep distinct modules and still propagate gradients through all of them.So the six-layer diagram above isn't wrong. It just describes the  in the system, not necessarily separate pieces of code anymore.Drones and other vehicles: what changesMost of my own work is in the air, so a few differences worth knowing:, and often unstructured, with no lanes and no road graph. Sampling planners (RRT*) and polynomial trajectory optimisation (minimum-snap) are more common than lattice planners.Dynamics are fast and unforgiving. A quadrotor can't stop instantly, and a planning delay of 100 ms at speed is a real distance.Compute and weight budgets are tight. You can't carry a car's GPU rack on a 250 g drone, so efficiency matters more than benchmark scores. GNSS jamming and spoofing are now a routine problem in contested airspace. That's exactly why we build vision-only navigation at FromSky.When I reverse-engineered a cheap RC drone to make it autonomous <!-- LINK: RC drone reverse-engineering article -->, the planner was the easy part to write and the hard part to trust. Every planning failure I hit traced back to state estimation, not the search algorithm.The case against everything I just saidIt would be easy to end with "end-to-end won, learn everything." The evidence is more mixed than that.The steelman for modular, rule-based planning: In the 2023 nuPlan planning challenge, a team showed that a simple rule-based planner (PDM-Closed, essentially lane-following with a carefully tuned speed and safety model) matched or beat much more complex learned planners in closed-loop evaluation. Separately, researchers showed that common open-loop benchmarks on nuScenes could be "solved" surprisingly well using little more than the vehicle's own past motion. Learned planners often look better on paper than on the road. Modular systems also have something end-to-end systems struggle with: you can inspect each stage, test it in isolation, and prove certain safety properties. Regulators and safety engineers care about that.The steelman for end-to-end: Hand-written rules don't scale to the long tail. Every edge case adds rules, and the rules start conflicting. A learned system improves with data, and the companies with the most driving data have bet heavily on it. Diffusion planners and RL fine-tuning are also directly attacking the weaknesses that rule-based baselines exposed. hybrid. Use learning where the world is too complex to write down, such as prediction, scoring, and generating candidate trajectories. Keep a verifiable layer underneath that can veto unsafe plans: collision checks, kinematic limits, hard safety rules. For safety-critical vehicles, and especially for anything airborne, I want a planner that can explain why it  crash.Path planning is the moment an autonomous system commits to an action. Everything upstream, including sensors, perception, localisation, and prediction, exists to make that commitment a good one. The algorithms are well understood. The open problems are about uncertainty, interaction with other agents, and trust.If you're starting out, implement A* on a grid, then Hybrid A*, then RRT* in a simulator. You'll learn more about why planning is hard from watching your own planner fail than from any survey, including this one.

SHA-256 hash chains, Merkle trees, boundary capture, and the engineering behind AgentLedgerAn AI agent just priced a deal, approved a transaction, or updated a customer record. Months later, someone asks a simple question: what did the agent actually do, and can you show that the record has not been changed since?That second question is easy to underestimate. Traditional automation is usually governed by predefined logic that can be inspected and tested. Agent decisions can depend on probabilistic model output and the state the agent saw at that moment. Re-running the same request later may produce a different path. That makes the contemporaneous record unusually important.I built AgentLedger to make changes to that recorded history detectable. This article is about the engineering behind it: structured action records, deterministic serialization, SHA-256 hash chains, Merkle sealing, verification, where capture happens, and the limits of what the design can prove.One framing note before the code. This does not replace observability tools like OpenTelemetry. Those tell an operator how the system ran and help you debug it. AgentLedger answers a different question: giving the organization a checkable record of what was captured about the autonomous actor’s actions. With an independent external anchor, that record can also be verified against a reference outside the system that stores it.Disclosure: I am the author of AgentLedger, the open-source project described in this article.Step 1: Capture the action as a structured recordEvery meaningful step submitted to AgentLedger becomes an action record. A record captures which agent acted, what kind of action it was, the input context, the output or business result, and the agent-provided rationale. AgentLedger uses a fixed set of action types so the record shape stays consistent across platforms.enum ActionType {
  QUERY,       // Agent reads or retrieves data
  UPDATE,      // Agent modifies existing records
  CREATE,      // Agent creates new records
  DELETE,      // Agent removes records
  DECISION,    // Agent makes a decision or selects a path
  ESCALATION,  // Agent escalates to a human operator
  TOOL_CALL,   // Agent invokes an external tool or API
  CALCULATION, // Agent performs a calculation
  VALIDATION  // Agent validates data against rules
}
The TypeScript library is deliberately simple to use. Start a session, record actions as they happen, then seal and verify it.import { AgentLedgerSession, ActionType } from '@vluthra/agent-ledger';

const session = new AgentLedgerSession({
  agentId: 'pricing-agent',
  platform: 'salesforce',
  initiator: 'quote-workflow',
});

session.record({
  agentId: 'pricing-agent',
  actionType: ActionType.CALCULATION,
  input: { basePrice: 200, discountPct: 15 },
  output: { finalPrice: 170 },
  reasoning: 'Applied Gold tier discount to base price',
});

const sealed = session.seal();
const result = session.verify();
console.log(result.valid); // true
The field called  is a recorded rationale supplied by the agent or workflow. It is not a claim that the framework captures a model’s hidden internal chain of thought. The point is to preserve the explanation the system chose to record alongside the inputs and output.Step 2: Canonicalize before you hashBefore you can reliably hash a record, you need one consistent way to turn it into text. That's what canonicalization means. A cryptographic hash is deterministic only if the bytes going into it are deterministic. Two objects can represent the same logical information and still serialize differently because field order, missing values, dates, or formatting differ. If serialization can drift, verification becomes unreliable. AgentLedger uses the same hashing-and-sealing model in two separate implementations: the platform-agnostic TypeScript core and a  implementation in Apex. In the Salesforce version, the canonicalization rule is explicit.It builds an ordered list of fields, serializes that list as JSON, and hashes the resulting string. The order is locked:sessionId, sequenceNumber, timestamp, agentId, actionType, inputContext, outputResult, reasoning, previousHash
The implementation also normalizes null string fields to an empty string before serialization. A missing sequence number falls back to 0. The timestamp is converted with Apex , and  produces the final canonical representation.List<String> parts = new List<String>();
parts.add(safeString(record.Audit_Session__c));
parts.add(String.valueOf(record.Sequence_Number__c != null ? record.Sequence_Number__c.intValue() : 0));
parts.add(safeString(String.valueOf(record.Action_Timestamp__c)));
parts.add(safeString(record.Agent_Id__c));
parts.add(safeString(record.Action_Type__c));
parts.add(safeString(record.Input_Context__c));
parts.add(safeString(record.Output_Result__c));
parts.add(safeString(record.Reasoning__c));
parts.add(safeString(record.Previous_Hash__c));

String canonical = JSON.serialize(parts);
return sha256(canonical);
One detail matters: the code does not arbitrarily trim the text inside input, output, or rationale fields. If someone changes whitespace inside one of those hashed fields, that is a content change and should change the hash. The deterministic part is the serialization structure, field order, and null handling.This format becomes part of the protocol. Change the field order or serialization rules after deployment and old records will hash differently. That is why canonicalization should be versioned deliberately rather than treated as an implementation detail that can be changed casually.Step 3: Link every record to the one before itA cryptographic hash is essentially a fingerprint of some data. Once the canonical representation exists, AgentLedger computes a SHA-256 hash for the record and includes the previous record’s hash in the next record. In simplified form, the records form a hash-linked sequence:Record 0: hash(content0 + null) -> H0
Record 1: hash(content1 + H0)   -> H1
Record 2: hash(content2 + H1)   -> H2
If record 1's content changes after the fact, its recomputed hash no longer matches its stored hash, so verification detects the modification. If someone also replaces record 1's stored hash with the newly computed value, record 2 still points to the original H1, so the chain linkage breaks. Rewriting one record cleanly therefore requires rewriting the downstream chain as well. The structure assumes append-only behavior, but it does not magically make the underlying database immutable.The verifier checks more than the current record hash. It also checks that sequence numbers start at zero and increase by one with no gaps, that the first record has no previous hash, and that each later Previous Hash matches the stored hash of the preceding record.Step 4: Seal the session under a Merkle rootA hash chain is good at showing sequence integrity. Think of the Merkle root as one fingerprint for the ordered list of record hashes in the session. Change one of those hashes and the root changes. The tree structure also lets you verify that an individual record belongs to the sealed session without supplying every other record.             Root Hash
             /         \
         H(0,1)       H(2,3)
         /    \       /    \
       H(0)  H(1)   H(2)  H(3)
When the session is sealed, AgentLedger takes the record hashes in sequence order, hashes adjacent pairs, duplicates the last leaf when the count is odd, and repeats the process until one root remains. That root is stored as the session fingerprint.A Merkle proof lets you verify that one record belongs to a sealed session using only the sibling hashes along its path to the root. That lets you verify one record without having to supply every other record in the session.Verification does not ask a flag whether the session is valid. It rebuilds the evidence from the stored records and compares the result with what was sealed. recompute each record hash from its current content. confirm each Previous Hash matches the prior record hash and sequence numbers have no gaps. rebuild the root from the record hashes and compare it with the sealed fingerprint. confirm the number of records still matches the count captured for the session.Those checks let the verifier distinguish a changed record, a broken sequence, and a count mismatch instead of returning one generic red light.A tamper test you can reproduceThe Salesforce implementation has a simple end-to-end test that demonstrates the behavior without any special tooling:Run an agent flow that creates a session, records actions, and seals it.Click . The session shows .Edit a hashed content field on one audit record, for example the recorded rationale.Run  again. The session shows  because the recomputed hash no longer matches the stored hash.Restore the field to its original value.Run  again. The session returns to .That last step is important. AgentLedger proves the current record set is consistent with the sealed fingerprint. By itself, it does not prove that a temporary alteration never happened. That distinction becomes important when you talk about custody and external anchoring.Where capture happens: move it to the boundaryThere is an obvious objection to everything above. If the agent has to call  itself, what stops it from simply not recording an action? A provenance trail the agent can opt out of is not worth much.The answer is to stop relying on the agent to record itself. In the MCP reference demo, capture does not live inside the agent’s logic. It lives in middleware that wraps the MCP client’s . Every tool request routed through the gateway, and every response or error returned through it, becomes a receipt before the run continues. Declared decisions are captured at defined checkpoints the same way. For calls routed through the gateway, the agent does not choose whether to record. Recording happens because the call is on the path.This also handles work that crosses systems. In the demo, one agent run touches five separate MCP servers, and because every call routes through the same gateway, all of it correlates into a single ordered, sealed session with one Merkle root. The record does not fragment just because the work spanned several services.Here is the honest boundary, and it matters. Every call that routes through the gateway is captured. That is a real guarantee, and it is far stronger than hoping the agent logged itself. It is not the same as proving the agent had no other path at all. An agent given a second client or an out-of-band channel could act outside the gateway, and the receipt would not know. So the accurate claim is that every call through the capture boundary is recorded, not that every action the agent could conceivably take is recorded. Capture is enforced for calls routed through that boundary. Guaranteeing there is no path around the boundary is a deployment problem, not something the cryptography settles.The part that is easy to get wrong: independenceHashing does not remove the need for a trust boundary. If the records and the Merkle root live in the same system under the same privileged administrator, someone with enough access could rewrite the records, recompute the hashes, recompute the root, and leave behind a self-consistent history that is still false.The core framework therefore gives tamper evidence against changes that are not accompanied by a full privileged rewrite. Stronger assurance requires the sealed fingerprint to leave the system that stores the records. The external anchor does not have to be a blockchain. Any independently controlled, tamper-resistant store, such as a transparency log or trusted timestamping service, could serve the same purpose.In the reference demo, I use Arbitrum Sepolia, a public test network outside the record store that provides an independently maintained reference for the sealed root. The only AgentLedger audit payload sent outside the enterprise boundary is the 32-byte Merkle root; the underlying business data and recorded rationale remain inside.The point is to put the fingerprint somewhere it cannot be silently rewritten along with the internal records. Anchoring addresses one specific weakness: retrospective privileged rewriting of both the records and the locally stored fingerprint. It does not solve completeness or source authenticity, and it remains a pluggable extension rather than something bundled into the core package.What it deliberately does not proveA provenance mechanism becomes less useful when it claims more than it can establish. These boundaries are part of the design: it detects changes to records that were submitted and sealed. Capturing at the boundary, as the reference demo does, enforces recording for every call that routes through the gateway, which is a real guarantee. It still does not prove the agent had no path around the gateway, so completeness depends on how capture is deployed, not on the cryptography. it protects submitted values after recording. It does not independently prove that a recorded output exactly matches the underlying business-system mutation. verification proves present consistency with the fingerprint. If an altered value is later restored, the record can verify again. it does not decide whether an agent was correct, unbiased, or compliant with a regulation. It preserves and verifies the recorded evidence.This is also why I use the term  rather than . The framework is built to make inconsistent changes detectable. Stronger guarantees depend on how the fingerprint is anchored and how capture is enforced.Design constraints worth stealingZero external dependencies in the TypeScript core: hashing uses the platform crypto primitive, which keeps the verification path small. identical logical inputs must map to identical bytes before hashing.One model, two implementations: the TypeScript package and the Salesforce-native implementation are separate codebases that share the same hashing-and-sealing pattern. in the reference demo, enforcing capture in middleware around the call, rather than trusting the agent to log itself, is the single biggest credibility gain.Append-only by convention: the framework creates audit records and verifies them later rather than updating them as part of normal operation. source authenticity, completeness beyond the boundary, multi-agent orchestration, and binding records to actual mutations are separate problems, stated openly rather than hidden as assumptions.AgentLedger is open source under the MIT license. The repository includes the TypeScript core, the Salesforce implementation, the MCP gateway demo that captures across five servers and anchors a real Merkle root to a public test network, the hashing and Merkle logic, and the roadmap for the harder problems that are not solved yet.If you run agents that make decisions that matter, try the failure path instead of trusting the happy path. Seal a session, verify it, change a recorded field, and verify again. Then look at the boundary conditions and decide what additional controls your environment needs.

I built  (learnanatomy3d.com), a free 3D human anatomy atlas that runs in the browser:~2,900 clickable structures across 11 body systems; This post covers the performance problems I hit while loading ~2.9 million triangles, and how I fixed them.Click any structure to see its name, Latin term, origin, insertion and action: the site, 14 locales, and the static anatomy and disease pages.three.js through react-three-fiber: the 3D viewer. (Z-Anatomy, BodyParts3D, Human Reference Atlas), fitted to realistic character bodies with custom Node and Blender tooling, packed into gzip'd binary chunks and streamed in two passes: body surface first, full anatomy after.Problem 1: the page froze for 7 seconds while loadingClicks, navigation, even the "I understand" button: nothing responded while the model loaded. The Long Tasks API showed one 7.3-second task and about 13.6 s of blocking in total.A CPU profile pointed at . On a shader's first use, three.js reads its info log to report errors. Reading that log forces the browser to wait for compilation to finish, so KHR_parallel_shader_compile never gets the chance to help.Fix 1: turn the check off in production, when the renderer is created. react-three-fiber renders the environment map  runs, so setting the flag there is too late.<Canvas
  gl={(defaults) => {
    const renderer = new THREEWebGLRenderer({ defaults antialias: true });
    rendererdebugcheckShaderErrors = processenvNODE_ENV !== "production";
    return renderer;
  }}
/>
Fix 2: compile a model before it joins the scene. polls the driver instead of blocking. The model is only attached once its shaders are ready, and the loading screen stays up until then.await renderercompileAsync(modelroot camera scene); // still detached
setAttached(true);                                       // now render it
Problem 2: textures decoded twice on the main threadEach loading pass built its own materials, so every 2048² texture was decoded and uploaded , at about 150 ms each, on the main thread. decode with , which works off the main thread, and cache by URL so both passes share one texture:const cache = new Map<string Promise<THREETexture>>();
const loader = new THREEImageBitmapLoader()
  setOptions({ imageOrientation: "flipY" premultiplyAlpha: "none" });

function surfaceTexture(url: string) {
  if (!cachehas(url)) {
    cacheset(url loaderloadAsync(url).then((bitmap) => {
      const t = new THREETexture(bitmap);
      tflipY = false;          // already flipped by createImageBitmap
      tneedsUpdate = true;
      return t;
    }));
  }
  return cacheget(url)!;
}
(Older Safari and Firefox ignore , so they fall back to .)Problem 3: switching bodies rebuilt everythingSwitching male ↔ female unmounted the . That threw away the WebGL context, every compiled program and the environment map.  keep the canvas mounted. The old body stays under the loading screen until the new one is ready, so the click responds in about 0.1 s and compiled programs are reused.Problem 4: 2,900 objects shouldn't mean 2,900 draw callsEach body system is merged into . Every vertex carries a part index, and a small RGBA data texture holds the per-part state (visibility, highlight, opacity, explode offset). Selecting or hiding a structure is a texel write, never a re-merge.Cross-sections use one persistent clipping plane per material, parked far away when the cut is off. Turning the cut on never changes the number of planes, so it never recompiles shaders.Main-thread blocking during load: .Longest freeze: . What's left is the GPU driver's first-ever shader compile.Body switch: the click responds in .What I'd love feedback onHow does it perform on  phone, and is anything confusing? Try it: . It's free, needs no account, and works in 14 languages.Disclosure: I'm the developer of LearnAnatomy3D. The anatomy is real scientific data (Z-Anatomy, BodyParts3D, the Human Reference Atlas).
